$ErrorActionPreference='Stop'
$Here=Split-Path -Parent $MyInvocation.MyCommand.Path
$Source=Join-Path $Here 'staged'
$Updates=Join-Path $Here 'distribution\updates'
$Version=(Get-Content -Raw (Join-Path $Source 'app\release_info.json') | ConvertFrom-Json).app_version
$Exclude=@('tests','archive','distribution','.venv','__pycache__')
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force -Path $Updates|Out-Null
$Stage=Join-Path $Updates 'LabTools'
if(Test-Path $Stage){Remove-Item -LiteralPath $Stage -Recurse -Force}
Copy-Item -LiteralPath $Source -Destination $Stage -Recurse
Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath $Stage -Directory -Recurse -Force|
 Where-Object{$_.Name-in$Exclude}|Sort-Object FullName -Descending|
 Remove-Item -Recurse -Force
$Zip=Join-Path $Updates "WINK_App_Update_v$Version.zip"
if(Test-Path $Zip){Remove-Item -LiteralPath $Zip -Force}
Compress-Archive -LiteralPath $Stage -DestinationPath $Zip -CompressionLevel Optimal
$Hash=(Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 -LiteralPath $Zip).Hash.ToLowerInvariant()
[ordered]@{
 app_version=$Version
 package_filename=(Split-Path -Leaf $Zip)
 package_sha256=$Hash
 min_runtime_version='1.0.0'
 changelog='v11.122 Myocyte morphometry ported from the Fiji macro to a native Python tool, with the review step the macro never had. Validated against the macro's own historical output on disk rather than synthetic fixtures: cell geometry replayed on a real boundary polygon matches the ImageJ row that produced it to under 0.35 percent on every field (area, perimeter, Feret, MinFeret, major, minor, circularity, solidity, Feret angle), and the sarcomere detection chain reproduces the macro's own profile.txt debug exports bit-for-bit including a 319-sample profile with saturation spikes the smoothing must suppress rather than count. Three geometry formulas had to be derived rather than taken from the obvious library call - ImageJ's polygon perimeter is the edge-length sum (a rasterised estimator was off by over 4 percent), its ellipse fit applies a plus one twelfth per-axis pixel-discretisation correction (omitting it was off by about 3.5 percent), and its Feret angle negates dy because image Y runs downward. Fibre review now runs BEFORE saving rather than at save time, so traces can actually be corrected: click a fibre to cycle straight, wavy, low-confidence; right-click to delete a trace that jumped between two real fibres; draw a missed fibre by hand, which was previously impossible since the tracer only ever seeds from detected bands; or re-trace with an adjustable link distance, the knob that decides whether the tracer follows one fibre or hops onto its neighbour. Hand-drawn fibres are tagged manual and drawn dashed. New provenance columns wave_n_seeded, wave_n_manual, wave_n_relabelled and wave_link_um. DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE from the macro: wave_n_fibers and the wave_width_fraction denominator now count fibres actually traced and shown, not bands seeded - the macro counted seeds whose trace was too short to use, which is indefensible once a person reviews the visible set, so wave fractions will not match legacy macro rows exactly. Two ways a wrong number could reach the CSV looking correct are closed: a sampling line crossing no resolvable banding used to save silently as sarc_mode=AUTO with sarc_number=0, indistinguishable from a real measurement of zero sarcomeres, and scale-bar auto-detect used to apply the 1.0 mm default to a confocal bar printed in um - a roughly 20x error seen twice in real use, where a 904 px bar labelled 49.2 um read as 1.0 mm gives 1.10619 um/px instead of 0.05442. Measuring a bar now sets nothing; the printed value must be entered and applied with a second explicit click. Also adds myocyte body-wall identity Myo01-24 with next-number suggestion and the bundled numbering schematic, saved myocytes staying outlined and labelled on the image for the whole session, session save and resume, mouse-wheel zoom on the cursor, view-only display brightness and contrast that never touches measured pixels, and correction logging for every EDITED, MANUAL and MANUAL_RECOUNT sarcomere count joinable to its CSV row. The Fiji macro remains available as a legacy entry. Prior: v11.121 No-DIC GCaMP triage tools, and a segmentation that was erasing the animal. Two prototype tools for Pmyo-3 GCaMP recordings without a DIC channel are added under tools/single_channel_gcamp as separate modules - nothing merged into gcamp.py, nothing wired into the hub, both still below Experimental grade. Validating them against real lab data found that flatten_and_segment estimates the background with a Gaussian of sigma 25 px while the worm in these recordings is roughly 15 to 25 px wide, so the background estimate follows the animal and subtracts it away, leaving only the brightest core. Measured on real frames the corrected segmentation returns 32,209 px of body where the intensity threshold returned 2,787, and on a coiled frame 17,523 against 3,276 - gains of five to twelve times. At sigma 25 one frame did not merely under-cover the worm: the largest component returned was background noise along a frame edge. A second and deeper problem remains that sigma alone does not fix - Pmyo-3 GCaMP brightness varies along the body WITH muscle calcium, the signal being measured, so any intensity threshold captures a different fraction of the animal frame to frame and mask area becomes confounded with the biology, which is exactly what conserved-area calibration assumes it is not. A new opt-in segment_body_and_signal addresses both with a background sigma that does not eat the animal and three-population multi-Otsu splitting background, body and elevated signal, returning a body mask for morphology and a signal mask for calcium within it; it sits alongside flatten_and_segment rather than replacing it, since changing that default would silently alter every existing result, and its sigma is validated on one dataset only. The coil/self-overlap classification, implemented from the published method but never validated, no longer asserts itself: a matching frame now returns unverified_shape_change with the coil hypothesis preserved in its note and a warning that a two-worm collision has been mistaken for one before. Real coiled frames were found and archived as fixtures; what is still missing is a straight-then-coiled pair of the SAME animal. Also recorded: one sample folder contains 17 separate acquisitions of about 107 frames each, concatenated only by filename sort, so treating such a folder as one continuous recording compares different animals. A 31-check regression suite covers the classifier and body/signal separation against three real archived frames. No existing measurement changed. Prior: v11.120 Basal slowing: needs correction can now correct something. The review offered accept, reject, or flag as needing correction - but nothing in the module could act on that flag and nothing else did either, so a track that jumped between animals could be labelled and then stayed exactly as it was. Tracks can now be edited during review: join two fragments (shift-click a pair), split at the current frame, trim before or after it, delete a track, and undo. Two tracks that overlap in time cannot be joined, because things visible simultaneously are not one animal, and a refused join records nothing. Crucially, entry events are DERIVED from trajectories - when an animal crosses into a lawn and what falls inside the before and after windows - so an edited track whose events were not rebuilt would describe a trajectory that no longer exists. Editing therefore triggers a re-derivation, verified: splitting one track turns one entry event into two across two tracks. If the rebuild fails you are told plainly that the events still describe the ORIGINAL tracks rather than being left with stale ones. Every edit is written to manual_track_edits.json so a recomputed event can be traced to a decision. Two new engine entry points make this possible: derive_entry_events was extracted from analyze (145 lines lifted verbatim, every output byte-identical before and after), and recompute_events_from_tracks rebuilds events from saved or edited tracks without decoding a frame - which also means a finished run can be corrected for a wrong declared frame rate or scale, as Population tracking can. Visibility: the event map drew every trajectory in grey at 0.7 width and 60 percent opacity on blank white; it now draws the recording underneath (background_reference.png was already saved and simply unused), gives each animal its own colour at 1.6 width and 90 percent opacity, enlarges entry markers, and runs inside a review workbench so it is themed, screen-clamped and cannot hide behind the main window. In the track reviewer one colour scheme had been doing two jobs: trails now carry identity (one colour per animal, 2.2 width) because previously every undecided track in the open field was the same green, while position markers keep their region meaning - cyan in the start ROI, red on a lawn - with decision colours overriding once a call is made. The link distance is adjustable at last: max_link_px was fixed at 60 source pixels with no way to change it, and an over-large gate is exactly what lets a track jump across the plate. Measured on a real 7.5 fps recording animals move 0.42 px per frame at the median and 3.15 px at the 95th percentile, against a gate nineteen times larger; the field is now exposed with a Measure motion button that samples the recording and suggests a value. Note the v11.116 physical speed limit already applied here, since basal slowing shares the population tracking linker - so results produced before v11.116 will already improve on a re-run. review_tracks keeps its previous contract by default, so other callers are unaffected. Prior: v11.119 Nothing was checking the two numbers everything depends on. Frame rate and micrometres-per-pixel are typed in by hand in every module and recorded as declared; nothing verified either. Both propagate linearly into reported results - speed scales with BOTH, frequency with the frame rate alone - so a value wrong by a factor of four makes every result wrong by a factor of four, silently and plausibly. The case that prompted this: a recording captured at about 7.5 fps but written with a 30 fps header, analysed at 2.0 um/px when the true scale was nearer 11, reported 1.17 Hz and 124 um/s for crawling animals; the right figures are near 0.29 Hz and 155 um/s. Nothing objected, even though the module had already measured those animals at 100 px long - which at the declared scale means 200 um, the size of an L1 larva rather than the adults on the plate. Population tracking gains Stage (L1 through Adult day 5), Vessel (blank unless one is visible; 96/48/24/12/6-well plates and 3/5/6/10 cm dishes) and Expected mode, plus two independent ways to estimate scale: trace one animal head to tail and its stage length converts pixels to um/px, or measure a vessel of known size - the whole rim when it is in frame, or three or more points on a PARTIAL rim, since an arc determines the circle. The arc fit reports the angle it spans and grades itself good, weak or poor, because a short nearly straight arc barely constrains a radius. Every estimate is shown beside the declared value with the ratio; applying it is optional and declining still records it. After each run, results are compared against published ranges for the stated stage and mode and anything out of range is explained in the process hood, naming which declared parameter would produce it. Ranges are deliberately wide - they catch four- and five-fold mistakes, not biology - and nothing is ever corrected automatically. The lab can build its own sharper ranges: confirmed runs accumulate to a measurement library, but ONLY confirmed ones, because a library built from runs with wrong declared parameters would encode the mistakes as normal. A new action corrects a finished run: Correct the scale or FPS of a finished run re-derives a completed analysis under corrected values without decoding a frame, reusing detection, linking and spines (which depend only on pixels and frame numbers) and RECOMPUTING summaries and modality proposals rather than rescaling them, because the classifier compares frequency against fixed thresholds. Verified to reproduce a direct run with the correct values exactly while leaving the original byte-for-byte untouched; results go to a new folder and any review of the original does not carry over. Recordings also now carry a substrate reading inferred from background texture (seeded lawn versus clean agar) as measurements plus a tentative, clearly unvalidated label, and every run writes calibration_provenance.json recording every scale estimate, which was used, what disagreed and whether a person confirmed it - stored whether or not anyone acted on it, so the dataset can be re-examined later. No measurement changed. Prior: v11.118 Failures stop being invisible. No measurement changed. Basal slowing never wrote its decision manifest: write_decision_manifest was called with an undefined variable (minimum_window_s, which is not a parameter - the paired comparison is sized by before_s and after_s), and because the whole write sits inside a try/except that only records the error to a side file, the NameError was swallowed on every run since the line was written. decision_transparency.json, the module provenance record, was never produced and the only trace was a decision_transparency_error.txt stub nobody reads. It now records the parameters that actually exist; the analysis itself is unchanged and the regression test passes before and after. Second, Tk sends callback exceptions to stderr, which pythonw discards, so a button whose handler raises looks exactly like a button that does nothing - the failure mode that hid Population tracking dead Confirm/Relabel/Reject buttons until someone reported them by hand. CockpitApp now installs a handler that writes the failure into the process hood and status line with file and line number, which eleven tools inherit in one change: basal slowing, defecation/pBoc, egg counting, egg laying, failure library, population tap, myocyte morphometry, paralysis pharmacology, pharynx morphometry, Population tracking and single-channel GCaMP. Six plain tk.Tk tools get the same through a new shared install_error_reporting helper: pharyngeal pumping, mechanosensation, orientation workbench, population orientation, track-derived workbench and the RGBCaMP results browser, surfacing as a dialog where a tool has no status line. Every import is wrapped so a diagnostic convenience can never stop a working tool from starting. Also audited: Population tracking worst defect, where a new analysis silently loaded the previous run reviewed tracks, was checked against every other tool that reloads prior review state - the pattern occurs nowhere else, egg counting correctly merges prior marks with fresh detections, so no other tool needed changing. Still outstanding: basal slowing area gates default to 40/2500 source pixels, the same values that flood a 4K recording with noise, and the Measure a worm helper that fixes this is still specific to Population tracking. Prior: v11.117 Population tracking: the locomotion-modality classifier could never propose anything on recordings at 30 fps or above. Detailed spines are deliberately sampled at about 15 Hz, so at 30 fps a spine exists on at most every second frame - but the posture gate measured spine coverage against EVERY frame in the window and demanded 0.65 of it. That threshold was unreachable by construction, so every bout came back uncertain with confidence 0.00 and the reason "insufficient spine or track coverage", which was true but misleading. Measured on a real 30 fps recording, zero of 558 windows could pass the gate even with a skeleton that succeeded on 99.7 percent of attempted frames. Posture evidence is now measured against the frames where a spine was ATTEMPTED, which is what the sampling stride defines: on that same recording 439 of 558 windows now pass, and bout confidence rose from 0.000 to a median of 0.392. Those bouts are still labelled uncertain, but now because the evidence genuinely overlaps rather than because the classifier never ran. "Uncertain" also now says why: insufficient_track_coverage, insufficient_spine_evidence, possible_collision_in_window, no_usable_frequency, or overlapping_modality_evidence - five situations that were previously one uninformative label and that call for different fixes. Every modality window and every reviewed bout now records how much real posture evidence backs it: spine_frames_used, spine_frames_attempted, spine_stride_frames, spine_sampling_rate_hz and spine_fraction_of_all_frames, so a metric derived from a partial spine sample can state its sample. NOTE: spine_valid_fraction is now a fraction of ATTEMPTED frames, not of all frames, so it is not comparable with v11.116 and earlier; spine_fraction_of_all_frames keeps the old definition. On a 20 fps recording (stride 1) nothing changes; any recording at 30 fps or above will now produce different modality proposals because previously it could produce none, so re-run anything where the modality call mattered. Prior: v11.116 Population swimming is renamed Population tracking, its review now happens inside the tool window over the movie, and eight defects were fixed. IMPORTANT: re-analysing a recording used to silently show you the PREVIOUS run. Results land in a folder named after the recording, and the review preferred the earlier reviewed file over the detections just computed - so changing area gates, resolution or ROIs appeared to do nothing. On a real recording a fresh 5.8 MB detection set (about 20,000 rows) was discarded in favour of a 176 KB leftover holding 432 rows. A new analysis now archives any previous review into superseded_review_<timestamp>/ and uses the fresh detections; Resume existing results review still restores prior work. Also fixed: the spine skeletoniser could loop forever at 100% CPU on any mask that filled its own bounding box (reachable whenever a low-resolution proxy admitted tiny solid blobs), which is why runs sat on one track for 20+ minutes; the bout review Confirm/Relabel/Reject buttons silently did nothing on pandas 3 because an empty label column was read as float; Tk callback errors vanished into stderr and are now reported in the hood with file and line; and the tracker could weld separate animals into one track because it extrapolated position by velocity across long crossing holds - a link must now also satisfy a physical speed limit from the last OBSERVED position, and Max link is exposed (measured worm motion on a 4K recording is about 2-6 px per frame against a hard-coded gate of 60). Review is now one window instead of four: tracks are drawn over the movie and stay synchronised as you scrub or play, each accepted animal in its own colour, the selected track carries a large marker and every animal is labelled, and trail length is selectable. Tracks can be edited: shift-click or lasso any number, stitch N fragments in time order, split at a frame, delete, add missing points by clicking where the animal is, and fill gaps by interpolation. Points you place carry identity ONLY - they are flagged manual_point and excluded from speed, coverage, frequency and curvature, with detected_frames and manual_points reported per track. Two new buttons set the parameters from the recording instead of from a guess: Measure a worm reads the area the detector gives one animal you click, and Mark all animals records how many there are so the review can flag when tracking finds a different number. Areas are in SOURCE pixels, so the old defaults flooded 4K recordings with noise. Opening a compressed movie no longer decodes the whole file to count frames: 122.8 s became 4.3 s on a 126 MB 4K clip for interactive paths, while analysis still forces an exact count because a blank end frame sets coverage_fraction. The spine skeleton is now selectable and recorded in analysis_metadata.json: the historical default remains, but it fragments on thick masks (no usable spine on 48% of attempted frames in a real recording) while connected thinning does not - compare both on your own recording with tools/population_swimming/compare_spine_methods.py before changing what you report. Prior: v11.115 Population swimming no longer looks frozen at "Writing results and timing report". The locomotion-modality classifier ran with no progress output and re-parsed each frame spine curvature about four times over, so on a real population it stalled silently for minutes. It now parses each frame once (identical classifications, far less work) and reports progress per track (Classifying locomotion modality: N of M), and the tool shows when the review window is being built. No classification result changed. Prior: v11.114 Small usability fixes. Supervised segmentation (the g workbench in the single-worm tracker): the threshold and scrub sliders no longer swallow the arrow keys, so Left/Right step the bad-frame preview by one source frame, PgUp/PgDn by ten, and Home/End jump to the first/last frame. Defecation: added a scope note that the tool scores the pBoc (posterior body-wall contraction, the intestinal step), not the aBoc or the expulsion step driven by the anal depressor and sphincter. Sample planner: the WINK logo now appears in the page header. No measurement changed. Prior: v11.113 Single-channel GCaMP rebuilt around three jobs. Job 1 (body-wall GCaMP): assess whether the worm body is separable from the background well enough to infer its outline, spine, and kinematics even on dim relaxed-muscle frames, then hand off to the single-worm tracker. Job 2 (a single cell): mark the soma and the process tip toward the nose, and the cell is tracked with its soma-to-tip long axis, reporting per-frame position, brightness, elongation, translational speed, and angular velocity (rotation of that axis, not the direction of travel). Job 3 (cell plus body): both at once, recording cell brightness alongside a body-trackability check. A job selector switches the numbered buttons; results export as CSV and JSON. Other tools unchanged. Prior: v11.112 Bug fixes and usability from lab testing (no biological measurement formulas changed). Defecation/pBoc: the preview and calibration frame sliders now scroll smoothly instead of sticking; the File button loads a whole numbered image sequence instead of a single frame; arrow keys and press-and-hold on the frame buttons scroll continuously; Backspace or middle-click undoes the last outline point during calibration; and a new optional worm-focus ROI lets you draw a box that follows the worm across the movie so tracking ignores other worms and distractors. The process hood now logs each step. Single-worm tracker: the open-file dialog comes to the front, and choosing the analysis interval is now a visual slider (scroll and mark start/end) instead of a text prompt. Pharyngeal pumping: the guided ROI-trajectory review (button 3b) gained a frame slider and an explicit Add-anchor-here button. Single-channel GCaMP: picking one frame of an image sequence now loads the whole folder. Mechanosensation: renamed from Evoked mechanosensation and habituation; marking stimuli now loads the whole image sequence so stimulus times can be set, plus a new What-this-does panel and an example-CSV template. Sample planner promoted to Ready. Dialogs across these tools now open in front of the window. Prior: v11.111 GitHub-based auto-update. The in-app updater can now fetch updates from the WINK GitHub releases (agvg75/WINK) when the lab L: drive is not reachable, so people running WINK off the lab network get the same one-click update prompt as lab machines. Lab machines still update from L: first; GitHub is only the fallback. No tool behaviour changed. Prior: v11.110 Pharynx morphometry and single-channel GCaMP now use the shared cockpit layout: inputs and the numbered workflow buttons on the left, a first-frame preview and status in the center, process hood on the right, with the logo theme. Their calibration, template placement, feasibility pass, extraction, neuron tracking, and review are all unchanged. This completes the migration of the safe form tools. Prior: v11.109 Three more tools moved onto the shared cockpit layout with the logo theme: Dynamic egg laying, Population tap response / habituation, and Neuromuscular paralysis pharmacology now have their inputs and buttons on the left and instructions/status in the center. Their calibration, reference-egg marking, tap detection, prod-observation review, and analysis are all unchanged. Prior: v11.108 Egg counting migrated to the shared cockpit layout: the source, frame, calibration-distance, tolerance, and worm-length fields plus the Calibrate, Draw-region, Mark-egg, and Detect/Review buttons are on the left; the chosen frame and the advanced egg-detection dials are in the center; the process hood is on the right. Calibration, ROI drawing, egg marking, detection, and the live-refresh review behave exactly as before. Prior: v11.107 The tool windows now carry the WINK logo colors too: the cockpit tools (myocyte morphometry, defecation/pBoc, population swimming, basal slowing), pharyngeal pumping, egg counting, and the shared review / ROI-drawing windows all use the slate-blue and sage-green palette with a sage accent stripe, matching the hub. This is a visual theme only - no tool behaviour changed. Prior: v11.106 Hub restyled to the WINK logo colors: the maroon is replaced by the logo slate-blue for the wordmark and headings, with a sage-green top accent stripe. Added Illinois State University under Molecular Neuroscience Lab in the brand. Individual tool windows are unchanged (neutral). Prior: v11.105 Population basal slowing now runs on the shared cockpit layout: the image folder, FPS, scale, area gates, before/after windows, buffer, and fraction inputs plus the Draw-ROIs, Undo, Clear, Analyze, and Load/Save-ROIs buttons are on the left; the first frame of the chosen folder and the status are in the center; the process hood is on the right, with the c and h panel toggles. It also gains a Calibrate scale (scope / bar) button that opens the shared in-window scale panel. ROI drawing, tracking, track review, and the paired lawn-entry review are unchanged. Close and reopen the tool to update. Prior: v11.104 made the scale calibration in-window across the cockpit tools.'
}|ConvertTo-Json|Set-Content -LiteralPath (Join-Path $Updates 'update_manifest.json') -Encoding UTF8
Remove-Item -LiteralPath $Stage -Recurse -Force
Write-Output "Built application update: $Zip"
